# Patch 0081 — Tab assistant (ROADMAP Phase 6; ADR 0014). The last of Phase 6:
# three palette commands that act on a pile of open tabs — group them by topic,
# close the duplicates, save the session.
#
# TWO OF THE THREE NEED NO MODEL, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. "Close duplicate
# tabs" is a set operation over normalized URLs and "save this research
# session" is a snapshot; making either depend on a local model would dress
# deterministic work up as AI and break it for everyone without one. Only
# topic grouping genuinely needs a model — naming what a set of pages is ABOUT
# is the part no algorithm does — and it is the only one that reports "no local
# model found". Saving still works with no model; it just falls back from a
# suggested name to "<Space> — <date>".
#
# MODEL OUTPUT IS NEVER TRUSTED AS CONTROL FLOW. The grouping prompt asks for
# JSON and the parser treats the reply as a hostile string: find the first
# array, validate every index against the real tab list, drop what does not
# check out. Verified against replies that are fenced, prose-wrapped, missing
# a label, carrying out-of-range and negative indices, and plain refusals — a
# confused model changes NOTHING. It can produce a bad grouping; it cannot
# close, move or lose a tab.
#
# DECIDING AND DESTROYING ARE SEPARATE. planDuplicates() returns what would be
# closed and changes nothing; closeDuplicates() confirms, then acts. Pinned
# tabs are never closed and never count as the duplicate to remove — patch
# 0034's D0, a quit-time cleanup that destroyed pinned tabs, is the standing
# reminder of what ignoring that costs. Only the active Space is touched.
# The confirmation is injectable so the path can be exercised without a modal
# dialog blocking the harness; it defaults to the real prompt, so no caller in
# the browser can skip it by accident.
#
# SAVED SESSIONS SURVIVE RETENTION. KavachaSpaceHistory gains `label` (a real
# COLUMN, migrated onto existing profiles — listSnapshots renders the timeline
# and deliberately never parses payloads, which hold every tab's full
# SessionStore state) and `force`. A saved session is exempt from both the
# per-space cap and the age cutoff: count-and-age retention silently deleting
# something the user named would be data loss on a feature whose whole promise
# is "keep this". `force` exists because pressing Save and getting nothing back
# — because the structural hash matched the last automatic snapshot — is a bug,
# not de-duplication. The timeline shows the name instead of the reason.
#
# Zen's fork of gBrowser.addTabGroup calls insertBefore.before(group)
# unconditionally, so upstream's `insertBefore = null` default throws. Groups
# are inserted at their first tab, which is Zen's own convention.
#
